== Gemini's third pass on #711, 2026-11-06: five points
1. The step's reclaim: REAL, both cases. kvReclaim stops the borrower holding most past its share across every slot, and set a flag that aborted the step whatever slot that was: a reclaim of a borrower the step does not name aborted a step whose every row had mapped. And a slot in the step rewinding into its own share was stopped by another row's reclaim before its own write trimmed its borrowed tail, so its guaranteed write failed. Now kvMapStep first ends each of its slots' sequences past its last row (the trim its own writes would do), then maps within-share rows, then borrowers; a reclaim records the slot it stopped (kvReclaimed), and the step aborts only when that slot is one of its own. Red first: TestKVPages_stepReclaimOutsideItLaunches (ErrKVPoolFull from a step whose row reclaimed from slot 2, not in it) and TestKVPages_stepRewindTrimsFirst (slot 1 stopped before its rewind).
2. A write past the sequence: REAL. kvWrite now refuses lo > valid, so no write leaves the rows between pointing at block 0 while valid advances. UploadKV maps from min(base, valid): a windowed layer's base may lie past the rows the slot holds while the full layers upload from 0. kvWriteMust (setPos) maps from the sequence's end when its write starts past it: a no-op after an entry point, which leaves valid past pos, and it gives a test hook or kernel test that calls setPos at a depth of its choosing blocks of the slot's own. Red first: TestKVPages_writePastTheSequenceRefused (a prefill from 200 into a slot of 64 rows accepted, one more block held).
   The refusal found two tests the on-demand pool had made vacuous: kvSeedRows wrote its rows through a table no block was mapped in, so every row went to block 0 and the scrambled and in-order arms of TestKVPages_tiledRowsPastTheTileBound compared the same empty tables (it now maps each slot's blocks first); and G1a decoded at 256 on an empty slot for each fixture whose batched prefill declines, comparing gaps (it now fills a declined prefill by decoding, as the decoder does). G1a still compares 33 of 54 fixtures.
3. G2 at gemma3-1b, 128: TAKEN, below. No cell of the morning's reading is void by the heavy-load rule (load at a cell's start 0.89 to 3.47 against 4.0; free memory as the harness records it (free_mem_mb) 4,236 to 6,728 MB against the fit's need plus 2 GB, at most 3,379), so 0.942 is a reading, not noise to discard. This round changes the decode path (kvWrite's refusal, kvWriteMust), so every G2 cell is read again on the final build; the morning's attribution A/B (main 4db6cd19, without KV5-6, against the build: 1.016) already places the cost before this slice.
